==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|abrode||obsolete}}
* {{alter|en|[[overbroad]]||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
First attested in mid 13th century. From {{inh|en|enm|abrood||broadly widely scattered}}, from {{m|enm|a-||on, in}} + {{m|enm|brood||broad}}. Equivalent to {{prefix|en|a|broad}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbɹɔːd/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbɹɔd/}}
* {{a|cot-caught}} {{IPA|en|/əˈbɹɑd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abroad.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔːd|s=2}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# Beyond the bounds of a country; in foreign countries. {{defdate|from 1350–1470}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 9, 2013|author=Andrei Lankov|title=Stay Cool. Call North Korea’s Bluff.|work=New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/10/opinion/stay-cool-call-north-koreas-bluff.html?_r=0
|passage=A closer look at North Korean history reveals what Pyongyang’s leaders really want their near-farcical belligerence to achieve — a reminder to the world that North Korea exists, and an impression '''abroad''' that its leaders are irrational and unpredictable.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author=w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|title=The History of England from the Accession of James II|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2612/2612-h/2612-h.htm|volume=3|chapter=XIV|passage=Another prince, deposed by the Revolution, was living '''abroad'''.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} At large; widely; [[broadly]]; over a wide space. {{defdate|from 1150–1350}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=8}}</ref>
#: {{ux|en|A tree spreads its branches '''abroad'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1718|author=w:Matthew Prior|title=Solomon, and other Poems on several Occasions|passage=Again: The lonely fox roams far '''abroad''', / On ſecret rapine bend and midnight fraud; {{...}}}}
# {{senseid|en|unconfined}}{{lb|en|dated}} Without a certain confine; outside the house; away from one's abode. {{defdate|from 1150–1350)}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=p. 1650|author=w:John Evelyn|editor=William Bray|title=[[w:John Evelyn's Diary|Diary]]|publisher=Frederic Warne and Company|year_published=1818|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=0DgIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA207&dq=abroad|page=207|section=entry for 1650 July 7|passage=I went to St. James', where another was preaching in the court '''abroad'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1891|author=w:Rudyard Kipling|title=The Return of Imray
|passage=She spoke to Strickland in a language of her own, and whenever in her walks '''abroad''' she saw things calculated to destroy the peace of Her Majesty the Queen Empress, she returned to her master and gave him information.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1900|author=w:Charles W. Chesnutt|title=w:The House Behind the Cedars|chapter=1|passage=Was it so irreconcilable, Warwick wondered, as still to peal out the curfew bell, which at nine o'clock at night had clamorously warned all negroes, slave or free, that it was unlawful for them to be '''abroad''' after that hour, under penalty of imprisonment or whipping?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1953|author=w:Ian Fleming|page=34|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/casinoroyale00flem/page/34/mode/1up|chapter=6|title={{w|Casino Royale (novel)|Casino Royale}}|passage=There were very few people '''abroad''' and the two men standing quietly under a tree on the opposite side of the boulevard looked out of place.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Before the public at large; throughout society or the world; here and there; moving without restriction. {{defdate|from late 15<sup>th</sup> c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1654|author=Richard Whitlock|title=Zootomia; Or, Observations on the Present Manners of the English|passage=This ''Peece'', or ''Schisme'' of ''Suicisme'', and ''Selfishnesse'', hath spawned most of the ''Heresies'' and ''Schismes'', that are '''abroad''' in the World.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Mark|1|45|passage=But he went out, and beganne to publish it much, and to blase '''abroad''' the matter: insomuch that Iesus could no more openly enter into the citie, but was without in desert places: and they came to him from euery quarter.}}
# Not on target; [[astray]]; in [[error]]; confused; dazed. {{defdate|from early 19<sup>th</sup> c.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|sports}} Played elsewhere than one's home grounds.

